#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166634 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "25214773-e6cb-5663-95fe-a04408844902" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/id #uuid "e8353dba-48d7-5f4f-8ba5-97e4e22b6e44" :question/math-academy-id "e-9050" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x>0$. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\, \\rm{m/s}$ and the magnitude of its acceleration is $\\sin^2 x \\,\\rm{m/s}^2$. Given that the acceleration is directed toward $O$, what differential equation describes this motion?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3235/Source/Images/cd73ecc46cd29774b30dd48cc477c4d3.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $\\sin^2 x$ and is directed *toward* $O$, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} = -\\sin^2 x\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{dv}{dx} \\cdot \\frac{dx}{dt} =-\\sin^{2} x\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nv= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\nv \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x} = -\\sin^2 x.\\qquad \\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(v^2) = 2v\\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac12v^2\\right) = v\\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d x}\n$$\n\nwe can write our differential equation $(\\ast)$ as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac12v^2\\right) = -\\sin^2 x\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166635 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1320d414-11eb-58a9-b59f-a6f760e87578" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/id #uuid "49e02115-6211-564b-8221-d5835a98a064" :question/math-academy-id "e-9051" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$, away from a fixed origin $O$. When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $x \\,\\rm{m}$, its velocity is $v\\,\\rm{m/s}$ and its acceleration is $(2x + e^{x-2}) \\, \\rm{m/s}^2$. If $v = 4 \\, \\rm{m/s}$ when $x = 2 \\, \\textrm m$, find the velocity of $P$ when $x = 1 \\, \\textrm m$. Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3235/Source/Images/adcc30a786d5323ec9b2c92769b6d11e.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(2x + e^{x-2}) \\,\\rm{m/s}^2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} = 2x + e^{x-2}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t}= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right)\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right) = 2x + e^{x-2}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{1}{2}v^{2})dx &= ∫(2x + e^{x - 2})dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2 = x^2 + e^{x-2} + C\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nv = 4 \\, \\rm{m/s}\n$$\n\nwhen\n\n$$\nx = 2 \\, \\textrm m\n$$\n\nwe can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} &= 2^{2} + e^{2 - 2} + C \\\\\n8 &= 4 + 1 + C \\\\\nC &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}v^{2} &= x^{2} + e^{x - 2} + 3 \\\\\nv^{2} &= 2(x^{2} + e^{x - 2} + 3) \\\\\nv &= ± \\sqrt{2(x^{2} + e^{x - 2} + 3)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x$, we have that $v(x) \\gt 0$, so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv = \\sqrt{2\\left(x^2 + e^{x-2} + 3\\right)}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x = 1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv &= \\sqrt{2(1^{2} + e^{1 - 2} + 3)} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2(4 + e^{-1})} \\\\\n&\\approx 2.96m/s\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166644 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "550bb33b-c427-5667-977d-56ff8c6b458e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Displacement of a Particle as a Function of Time"}] :question/id #uuid "80b653d0-5dff-5621-86ee-e1fd336ad740" :question/math-academy-id "e-9455" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\geq 0$. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,\\textrm m$, the magnitude of its acceleration is $\\dfrac{2}{(x+1)^2}\\, \\rm{m/s}^2$ and is directed toward $O$. Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O$ and has velocity $v=2\\,\\rm{m/s}$. Find $x$ as a function of time $t$." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3235/Source/Images/10ca9059211ec926ef3e8403f61e367f.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{(x+1)^2}\\, \\rm{m/s}^2\n$$\n\nand is directed *toward* $O$, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} =-\\dfrac{2}{(x+1)^2}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t}= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right)\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right) = -\\dfrac{2}{(x+1)^2}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{1}{2}v^{2})dx &= ∫ - (2)/((x + 1)^{2})dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}v^{2} &= \\frac{2}{x + 1} + K\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nv=2\\,\\rm{m/s}\n$$\n\nwhen\n\n$$\nx=0\\, \\textrm{m}\n$$\n\nwe can find the value of the constant $K$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}v^{2} &= \\frac{2}{x + 1} + K \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2} &= \\frac{2}{0 + 1} + K \\\\\n2 &= 2 + K \\\\\nK &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}v^{2} &= \\frac{2}{x + 1} \\\\\nv^{2} &= \\frac{4}{x + 1} \\\\\nv &= ± \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x + 1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x$, we have that $v(x) > 0$, so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x) = \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{x+1}}\n$$\n\nNow, we know that\n\n$$\nv= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{x+1}}\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x + 1}} \\\\\n\\sqrt{x + 1}\\frac{dx}{dt} &= 2 \\\\\n∫\\sqrt{x + 1}\\frac{dx}{dt}dt &= ∫2dt \\\\\n∫\\sqrt{x + 1}dx &= ∫2dt \\\\\n\\frac{2}{3}(x + 1)^{3/2} &= 2t + C_{1} \\\\\n(x + 1)^{3/2} &= 3t + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nC = \\dfrac{3C_1}{2}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0$. Therefore, $C=1$, and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n(x+1)^{3/2} =3t+1\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 1)^{3/2} &= 3t + 1 \\\\\nx + 1 &= (3t + 1)^{2/3} \\\\\nx &= (3t + 1)^{2/3} - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167000 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1320d414-11eb-58a9-b59f-a6f760e87578" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6b8daa4-a421-5ee6-82b9-9eb043ad7cf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x) = 2\\sqrt{x^{2} + 12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163419} {:answer/id #uuid "ca01366f-9c8a-51ce-8f15-bed65200a620" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x) = 2\\sqrt{x^{2} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163420} {:answer/id #uuid "cbe873e5-d7ad-55ae-91e9-d36486402d46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x) = 2x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163421} {:answer/id #uuid "13d41fac-776e-5c91-a0ac-84fdd7cd9133" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x) = \\frac{8}{5}\\sqrt{x^{2} + 21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163422} {:answer/id #uuid "20b8760e-1b1a-5405-bed3-21cdbc66936b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x) = x^{2} + 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163423}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6b8daa4-a421-5ee6-82b9-9eb043ad7cf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x) = 2\\sqrt{x^{2} + 12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163419} :answer-field/id #uuid "4eed9cef-29c7-5b9e-b2e1-4fece31a3301" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163418}] :question/id #uuid "cfc6326b-3536-5d27-a880-335a706849db" :question/math-academy-id "q-107999" :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nA particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $xm$, its velocity is $vm/s$ and its acceleration is $4xm/s^{2}$. If $P$ has a displacement of $2m$ when its velocity is $8m/s$, find $v$ in terms of $x$." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167001 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1320d414-11eb-58a9-b59f-a6f760e87578" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4448af6-7baf-5a5b-b8d1-39a077ebf8f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.1m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163425} {:answer/id #uuid "c9857de1-4db2-5ad9-a25d-dde1b667afaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.7m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163426} {:answer/id #uuid "f1bea300-34aa-57f5-afe8-1a6e6ecf1c64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.2m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163427} {:answer/id #uuid "2f61cdd9-b058-5af6-b23f-8b8773806d8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.8m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163428} {:answer/id #uuid "4113fec4-4e93-5ab7-8a45-bc6f10d75a97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.4m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9857de1-4db2-5ad9-a25d-dde1b667afaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.7m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163426} :answer-field/id #uuid "086d497a-6cd2-50e5-a303-5b7f3ca50234" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163424}] :question/id #uuid "9c92f4b3-2f0f-5819-981c-8fa3d450f8a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-108002" :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nA particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$, away from a fixed origin $O$. When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $xm$, its velocity is $vm/s$ and its acceleration is $(2 + 3e^{-x})m/s^{2}$. If $v = 2m/s$ when $x = 0m$, find the velocity of $P$ when $x = 3m$. Round your answer to one decimal place." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167003 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "25214773-e6cb-5663-95fe-a04408844902" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "832f5079-56a5-5633-a36a-abcaa350c6f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{1}{2}v^{2}) = 6x^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163437} {:answer/id #uuid "ece83403-1738-5762-a14b-2869fedf272b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dx} = 6x^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163438} {:answer/id #uuid "032ae97f-c542-562e-bb45-fad43b5fc7cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(v^{2}) =-6x^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163439} {:answer/id #uuid "342bdb71-912c-58bd-a61f-2bf43bf15640" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dx} =-6x^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163440} {:answer/id #uuid "366f26c4-1e76-5037-be3b-fe4e333ae3b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{1}{2}v^{2}) =-6x^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163441}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "366f26c4-1e76-5037-be3b-fe4e333ae3b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{1}{2}v^{2}) =-6x^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163441} :answer-field/id #uuid "5043cace-708e-584e-9f93-2239e526e2c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163436}] :question/id #uuid "cde0f0e6-e59d-5767-8e42-f1f093f582b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-108010" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x > 0$. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $vm/s$ and the magnitude of its acceleration is $6x^{3}m/s^{2}$. Given that the acceleration is directed toward $O$, which differential equation describes this motion?"}]}
